From: mbt28 <btekbas@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: suniv-di: run the module clock at 148.5 MHz

The de-interlacer (0048) left its module clock at the reset divider,
which makes it pll-video itself: 297 MHz on the F1C200s. There it puts
isolated wrong pixels on the interpolated lines (0, or about twice the
neighbourhood, where both fields, the previous frame and the vertical
average agree), and not the same ones from run to run.

Measured on the board, isolated outliers in 30 film frames with the
manual's DI_PARA defaults:

  module clock    stray pixels
  297 MHz             131-139   (display on or off, LEHU running or not)
  148.5 MHz             0
  74.25 MHz             0

At 148.5 MHz the composite camera is clean too (0 in 10 frames, against
~30 per frame), the motion quality is unchanged (36.94 dB against the
true progressive frame of a 50 fps test pattern) and 50 frames still
take 1.3 s, input and output included.

Set the rate on every resume, as a divider of pll-video, which the LCD
controller owns and which is therefore not changed.
---
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/suniv-di/suniv-di.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/suniv-di/suniv-di.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/suniv-di/suniv-di.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/suniv-di/suniv-di.c
@@ -59,6 +59,15 @@
 #define SUNIV_DI_FLAG_ADDR		0x70
 #define SUNIV_DI_PARA			0x80
 
+/*
+ * The module clock. Left at the reset divider it is pll-video itself, 297
+ * MHz, and the de-interlacer then writes isolated wrong pixels (0, or
+ * about twice the neighbourhood) on the interpolated lines, differently
+ * from run to run: ~4 per frame on film, ~30 on the composite camera. At
+ * 148.5 MHz there are none, and 720x576 still takes well under a field.
+ */
+#define SUNIV_DI_MOD_RATE		148500000
+
 #define SUNIV_DI_MIN_WIDTH		32U
 #define SUNIV_DI_MIN_HEIGHT		32U
 #define SUNIV_DI_MAX_WIDTH		720U
@@ -775,8 +784,9 @@
 }
 
 /*
- * The module clock is left at whatever rate it has: its parents include
- * pll-video, which the LCD controller depends on.
+ * The module clock is set on every resume, as a divider of whatever
+ * pll-video runs at: the LCD controller owns that PLL, so its rate is not
+ * changed (the clock has no CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT).
  */
 static int suniv_di_runtime_resume(struct device *device)
 {
@@ -791,6 +801,10 @@
 	if (ret)
 		goto err_rst;
 
+	ret = clk_set_rate(dev->mod_clk, SUNIV_DI_MOD_RATE);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_warn(dev->dev, "cannot set the module clock: %d\n", ret);
+
 	ret = clk_prepare_enable(dev->mod_clk);
 	if (ret)
 		goto err_bus;
